# Gitleaks fingerprint allowlist for verified non-secrets already in history.
#
# gitleaks scans COMMITS, not the working tree (secret-scan.yml checks out with
# fetch-depth: 0 and the action scans the PR's commit range). Renaming a bad
# fixture in a follow-up commit therefore does NOT clear the finding -- the
# original commit is still in range. Fingerprints are the supported way to
# retire such a finding, and they are exact: <commit>:<path>:<rule>:<line>.
# They cannot mask a finding in any other commit, file, rule, or line.
#
# Do NOT use this file for a real credential. A real credential must be
# rotated and the history purged, not ignored.
#
# Do NOT quote a key-shaped literal in this file, not even inside a comment.
# gitleaks scans this file like any other; a quoted example becomes a new
# finding in the commit that adds it, which is exactly how the second entry
# below came to exist.
#
# NOTE: the global [[allowlists]] blocks in .gitleaks.toml are NOT a substitute
# for this file. gitleaks 8.24.3 (pinned by gitleaks-action@v3) silently
# ignores the plural array-of-tables form when [extend] useDefault = true;
# only the singular [allowlist] table is honored. See the note on that file.

# 1. Test fixture, never a credential. The stripe checkout route test set
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY to a placeholder string to exercise the route's
# non-empty-after-trim configuration check. It was never a valid Stripe key and
# never authenticated anything. A later commit on this branch renamed it so no
# new commit carries a key-shaped literal; this retires the original commit.
686b5238cfa99c3d213a37fd51670d266e92938e:src/app/api/stripe/checkout/__tests__/route.test.ts:stripe-access-token:59

# 2. This file's own first revision, which quoted the fixture from entry 1 in a
# comment while explaining it. The quote was removed in the following commit.
# No credential was ever involved.
